# Explore Arithmetic on Hyperbolic Orbits

A determinant-one Möbius motion turns geometry into the recurrence $u_0=2$, $u_1=t$, and $u_{n+2}=tu_{n+1}-u_n$. Start by generating an orbit and watching its exact integer growth.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

Use the complete numerical suite to compare recurrence values with matrix powers and test all identities.

{{demo:0}}

## Multiplying time with polynomials

Doubling an index applies $x\mapsto x^2-2$; tripling applies $x\mapsto x^3-3x$. Build long jumps interactively, then inspect two computational approaches.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the algebra behind index multiplication</summary>

Cayley–Hamilton gives $A^2-(\operatorname{tr}A)A+I=0$ for a determinant-one $2\times2$ matrix. Taking traces yields $\operatorname{tr}(A^2)=\operatorname{tr}(A)^2-2$. Applying this to $A^n$ proves $u_{2n}=u_n^2-2$. The cube follows similarly, and $(A^n)^m=A^{mn}$ explains the general composition law.

For background, see [Möbius transformations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M%C3%B6bius_transformation) and [Chebyshev polynomials](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev_polynomials).
</details>

## The Pell-conic shadow

Every pair $(u_n,u_{n+1})$ remains on $x^2-txy+y^2=4-t^2$. The following orbit plot makes this Diophantine constraint visible.

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the discriminant factorization</summary>

Substitution into the doubling formula gives $u_{2n}^2-4=(u_n^2-4)u_n^2$. Thus doubling multiplies the old discriminant by a square. This offers an arithmetic signature of repeated powers. Read more about the broader setting of [Pell equations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pell%27s_equation).
</details>

## Growth and exact computation

Hyperbolic traces grow exponentially with the index. A logarithmic vertical scale reveals the rates for several initial traces.

{{visualization:0}}

{{algorithm:2}}

## Finite rings turn growth into cycles

Modulo $q$, the pair update is invertible, so every orbit is periodic from its first state. Explore individual cycles and compare periods across moduli.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal why there is no preperiod</summary>

The update $(x,y)\mapsto(y,ty-x)$ has inverse $(x,y)\mapsto(tx-y,x)$. It is therefore a permutation of the $q^2$ residue pairs. A trajectory of a permutation starts on a cycle; it cannot have a transient tail. This connects the recurrence to the finite group $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z/q\mathbb Z)$.
</details>

## Where the geometry leads

Trace is invariant under conjugation and obeys $|\operatorname{tr}A|=2\cosh(\ell/2)$ for a hyperbolic transformation of length $\ell$. This makes it a canonical coordinate for studying primitive closed geodesics. Future work asks for general multiplication polynomials, sharp modular periods, a classification of primitive Pell points, and effective primitive-geodesic counts. For geometric context, visit the [Poincaré disk model](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poincar%C3%A9_disk_model).

